  "mode": "verify",
  "problem": "Compute the arc length $L$ of $y=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{8}+\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}}$ for $x\\in [1,2].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$L=$ \n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{\u2032}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{8}+\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}},$ $a=1,$ and $b=2.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2032}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{\u2032}(x)]^{2}}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{\u2032}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}}]}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{6}-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{4{x}^{6}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{6}+\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{4{x}^{6}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{\u2032}(x)]^{2}}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}}$ to the formula for the arc length, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{\u2032}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{4}-\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}})|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{8}(2)^{4}-\\frac{1}{4(2)^{2}}]-[\\frac{1}{8}(1)^{4}-\\frac{1}{4(1)^{2}}] \\\\  & =\\frac{31}{16}-(-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{33}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
